<body>
<div class="container">

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
  <h1>Generative AI Competitive Landscape</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">User Sentiment &amp; Complaint Analysis Across Top 5 AI Apps — Q1 2026</div>
  <div class="meta">Product Strategy Team &bull; {r['total_reviews']} user reviews analyzed &bull; Source: Google Play Store &bull; April 2026</div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Executive Overview</h2>

  <p>This report analyzes <strong>{r['total_reviews']}</strong> user reviews from five leading generative AI mobile applications — ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Claude — sourced from the US Google Play Store in early 2026. The analysis covers sentiment patterns, thematic complaint breakdowns, sentiment-rating divergence, and review engagement signals.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">{r['best_star_val']}</div>
      <div class="label">Highest Avg Star Rating</div>
      <div class="detail">{r['best_star_app']}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">{r['highest_complaint_val']}%</div>
      <div class="label">Highest Complaint Rate</div>
      <div class="detail">{r['highest_complaint_app']}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">{r['highest_mismatch_val']}%</div>
      <div class="label">Highest Sentiment Mismatch</div>
      <div class="detail">{r['highest_mismatch_app']}</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Top 3 Strategic Takeaways</h3>
  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li><strong>Microsoft Copilot dominates user satisfaction</strong> — with the highest average star rating (4.24) and sentiment polarity (0.487), Copilot leads across both quantitative measures. Its lowest complaint rate (9.7%) and lowest mismatch rate (8.0%) signal genuine, not inflated, satisfaction. Copilot's free-tier generosity is a key driver.</li>
    <li><strong>Pricing is the #1 pain point across the ecosystem</strong> — ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Claude all see Pricing/Subscription as their top specific complaint theme. Users perceive rate limits and paywalls as aggressive. This is a competitive vulnerability for any app that monetizes too aggressively before establishing indispensable value.</li>
    <li><strong>Claude and Perplexity face trust headwinds</strong> — both apps show the highest sentiment-rating mismatch rates (~13.5%), meaning user star ratings and review text often contradict each other. Combined with Claude's 17.8% complaint rate (highest overall), these signals suggest users experience friction that simple rating averages understate.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: CROSS-APP SENTIMENT COMPARISON -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Cross-App Sentiment Comparison</h2>

  <p>We compare apps on two independent satisfaction axes: user-assigned star ratings (explicit) and VADER sentiment polarity derived from review text (implicit). Divergence between these measures reveals where self-reported ratings may not tell the full story.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>App</th><th>Avg Star Rating</th><th>Mean Sentiment</th><th>Complaint Rate</th><th>Mismatch Rate</th><th>Avg Thumbs Up</th><th>Avg Word Count</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {r['kpi_table_rows']}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_avg_star']}" alt="Average Star Rating by App">
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_avg_sent']}" alt="Mean Sentiment Polarity by App">
  </div>

  <p>Microsoft Copilot leads both measures decisively — its 4.24 average star rating is 0.43 points above the nearest competitor (ChatGPT at 3.81). Google Gemini and Perplexity cluster at the bottom (3.51 and 3.45 respectively), with Claude at 3.79. Sentiment polarity rankings mirror star ratings, confirming that Copilot's lead is genuine and not an artifact of rating inflation.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_star_dist']}" alt="Star Rating Distribution by App">
  </div>

  <p>Rating distributions reveal that Copilot has a notably heavier 5-star concentration. Google Gemini and Perplexity show bimodal distributions with significant 1-star clusters, indicating polarized user experiences.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: THEMATIC COMPLAINT ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Thematic Complaint Analysis by Application</h2>

  <p>Each review is tagged with one of four themes: General (no specific complaint), Pricing/Subscription, Bugs/Performance, or Accuracy/Logic Issues. The non-General themes represent identifiable pain points.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>App</th><th>General</th><th>Pricing / Subscription</th><th>Bugs / Performance</th><th>Accuracy / Logic</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {r['theme_table_rows']}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_theme_dist']}" alt="Review Theme Distribution by App">
  </div>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Claude has the highest total complaint rate at 17.8% of all reviews, with Pricing/Subscription as its dominant pain point. Perplexity follows at 15.9%, also driven primarily by pricing frustrations.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_complaint_stack']}" alt="Complaint Theme Breakdown (Excl. General)">
  </div>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Complaint Theme Highlights</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pricing/Subscription</strong> is the top complaint for ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Claude. Users cite rate limits, aggressive upselling, and lost subscription access as top frustrations.</li>
    <li><strong>Bugs/Performance</strong> hits Google Gemini hardest, with users reporting image generation failures, crashes, and response truncation.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy/Logic Issues</strong> is Microsoft Copilot's top specific complaint — despite leading overall satisfaction, users flag hallucinations and ignored instructions, particularly in image generation.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> faces a balanced spread of all three complaint types, suggesting systemic rather than isolated issues.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: SENTIMENT-RATING DIVERGENCE -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Sentiment-Rating Divergence &amp; Sarcasm Detection</h2>

  <p>A "mismatch" is defined as a review where the star rating and VADER sentiment polarity contradict each other: a 4-5 star review with negative sentiment (polarity &lt; -0.05), or a 1-2 star review with positive sentiment (polarity &gt; 0.05). These mismatches often signal sarcasm, mixed emotions, aspirational ratings, or Unicode text that VADER cannot parse.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>App</th><th>Total Mismatches</th><th>High Star / Neg Sentiment</th><th>Low Star / Pos Sentiment</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {r['mismatch_table_rows']}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_mismatch']}" alt="Sentiment-Rating Mismatch Rate by App">
  </div>

  <p>Claude and Perplexity show the highest mismatch rates (~13.5-13.6%), meaning roughly 1 in 7 reviews sends mixed signals. Microsoft Copilot's 8.0% mismatch rate — the lowest — further validates that its high star ratings reflect genuinely positive text, not rating inflation or sarcastic praise.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Implication:</strong> For apps with high mismatch rates, star-rating averages overstate true satisfaction. Product teams should weight text-derived sentiment alongside ratings when benchmarking against competitors, particularly for Claude and Perplexity.
  </div>

  <p>The "Low Star / Positive Sentiment" pattern (users leaving 1-2 star ratings with positive review text) is notably prevalent across all apps. This often reflects users who like the product concept but are frustrated by specific limitations — a recoverable segment if the core issues (typically pricing or rate limits) are addressed.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: REVIEW DEPTH & EMOTIONAL INTENSITY -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Review Depth &amp; Emotional Intensity</h2>

  <p>This section examines whether dissatisfied users write longer reviews and whether review engagement (thumbs-up votes) correlates with length or sentiment.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_words_star']}" alt="Review Length by Star Rating & App">
  </div>

  <p>A consistent pattern emerges across all five apps: <strong>1-star reviews are longer than 5-star reviews.</strong> Claude users write the longest reviews overall (avg 31 words), while ChatGPT and Perplexity users write the shortest (avg 26 words). The word count–sentiment correlation is weakly negative across all apps (r = -0.035 to -0.065), confirming that negative experiences drive more detailed feedback.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{r['chart_engage_theme']}" alt="Review Engagement by App & Theme">
  </div>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Engagement Patterns</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> receives significantly more thumbs-up engagement per review (avg 3.48) than any competitor — more than double Google Gemini's 1.91 and far above ChatGPT's 0.42.</li>
    <li>Word count–engagement correlation is positive but modest across all apps (r = 0.10–0.17), meaning longer reviews do attract somewhat more votes.</li>
    <li>Pricing-themed reviews and bug reports tend to draw higher engagement, suggesting these topics resonate strongly with other users browsing reviews.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <p>The following recommendations are prioritized by potential impact on competitive positioning and user retention, synthesized from all preceding analysis.</p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">1</div>
      <h4>Rethink Rate Limits &amp; Free-Tier Value</h4>
      <p>Pricing is the #1 complaint for ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Claude. Microsoft Copilot's satisfaction lead is partly driven by perceived free-tier generosity. Consider expanding free usage limits or restructuring paywalls to reduce friction before users establish daily habits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">2</div>
      <h4>Invest in Stability for Mobile</h4>
      <p>Google Gemini and Claude both suffer from Bugs/Performance complaints (crashes, server errors, image gen failures). Mobile-first reliability is table stakes — these issues drive 1-star reviews that are hard to reverse.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">3</div>
      <h4>Monitor Sentiment-Rating Divergence as a Leading Indicator</h4>
      <p>Apps with high mismatch rates (Claude 13.6%, Perplexity 13.5%) have inflated star averages. Track text-derived sentiment as a complement to ratings — it surfaces dissatisfaction earlier than rating declines.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">4</div>
      <h4>Target the "Like It But Frustrated" Segment</h4>
      <p>Low-star reviews with positive sentiment text represent recoverable users. These users appreciate the product but hit specific walls (rate limits, bugs). Proactive outreach or limit-increase trials could convert them to retained users.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">5</div>
      <h4>Study Copilot's Engagement Advantage</h4>
      <p>Copilot reviews receive 2-8x more thumbs-up engagement than competitors. This signals a more active reviewer community and potentially stronger word-of-mouth. Investigate whether Copilot's review prompting UX or user base composition drives this.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="num">6</div>
      <h4>Improve Accuracy Messaging for All Apps</h4>
      <p>Accuracy/Logic complaints appear across every app. Users specifically cite hallucination and ignored instructions. Setting expectations around AI limitations and providing confidence signals in responses could mitigate these complaints.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- FOOTER -->
<div class="report-footer">
  Generative AI Competitive Landscape Report &bull; Q1 2026 &bull; {r['total_reviews']} reviews analyzed &bull; Data: Google Play Store (US, English) &bull; Produced for Product Strategy Team
</div>

</div>
</body>
</html>"""

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"Report written: {len(html):,} chars")
